counterfactuals.output:
A theoretical reanalysis of x‑ray circular dichroism in Bi\(_2\)Sr\(_2\)CaCu\(_2\)O\(_{8+\delta}\) argues that the measured difference between left- and right-circularly polarized absorption is not, by itself, proof of a parity-breaking electronic phase. Using the low-symmetry, structurally modulated Bi2212 lattice, the authors show that ordinary anisotropic—or linear—dichroism can leak into the nominal circular-dichroism channel through imperfect polarization, sample misalignment, beam drift, or unequal structural domains. Signals produced this way can have the same magnitude and temperature dependence as those attributed to inversion- or mirror-symmetry breaking.

The study therefore does not rule out parity-breaking order, but concludes that the existing absorption measurements are not unique evidence for it. Suggested tests include:

- rotating the crystal azimuthally and checking the predicted symmetry dependence;
- reversing the beam direction or measuring opposite crystal faces;
- exchanging or carefully characterizing structural/twin domains;
- performing complete polarization analysis to separate linear from circular dichroism;
- tracking the signal systematically across the pseudogap temperature and with doping;
- using complementary symmetry-sensitive probes such as resonant x‑ray diffraction or optical second-harmonic generation.

A genuine parity-odd signal should transform consistently under these reversals and appear in more than one experimental channel.
